WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.770
Welcome to place value and estimation.

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.420
A number close to a round number can be easier to tackle

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:07.410
in two pieces.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.550
Work with the round part first,

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.210
then account for the difference.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:13.700
Keep an estimate in mind to check the size

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:14.390
of the answer.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:19.040
In AMC Upper Primary practice, an estimate can rule out

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.150
options whose sizes make little sense.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.100
When a Rossmoyne style problem asks for an exact

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.480
difference, keep the small adjustments visible so every

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.270
bit of the answer is accounted for.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:33.300
Add three thousand nine hundred and eighty-six and two

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.470
thousand and forty-seven.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.670
First think about the total to the nearest thousand.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:40.880
Then see whether you can get the exact sum without

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.330
lining up every digit.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.310
The nearby thousands are four thousand and two thousand,

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:51.870
giving an estimate of six thousand.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.560
The first number is fourteen below its round number.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:56.700
The second is forty-seven above.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.530
The total is therefore thirty-three above six thousand,

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:02.180
giving six thousand and thirty-three.

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:05.280
Multiply forty-nine by twenty-eight.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.300
Imagine forty-nine rows of twenty-eight squares.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.550
How could we turn that shape into a rectangle whose area

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.000
is easier to work out?

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:15.700
Remember to keep track of any row you add.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.290
Add one row to make fifty rows.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:23.870
Fifty times twenty-eight is fourteen hundred,

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.530
since fifty is half of a hundred.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.150
The extra row contains twenty-eight squares.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.520
Take it away and get one thousand three

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:32.450
hundred and seventy-two.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.280
An estimate of about fifteen hundred puts it in

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:37.620
the right neighbourhood.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.410
A theatre has thirty-seven rows of forty-eight seats.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:44.840
The owner adds four seats to each existing row and

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.170
builds two new rows.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.930
Each new row has fifty-two seats.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.450
After taking two hundred and fifty additional bookings,

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.730
how many of the added seats are still free?

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.240
Start with the old seating block.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:03.260
Attach four seats to each of the thirty-seven rows.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.340
That strip has one hundred and forty-eight seats.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.780
Now attach the two new rows across the full width.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:13.220
Two times fifty-two gives one hundred and four seats,

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:14.300
including the corner.

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:16.160
Add the two strips.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:19.170
One hundred and forty-eight plus one hundred and four is

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:19.620
two hundred and fifty-two.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.060
Remove two hundred and fifty for the bookings.

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:24.620
Two seats are free.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.650
You can also check by subtracting the old total from

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:28.340
the new total.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.750
If you extend all thirty-nine rows by four and also

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.030
count two new rows of fifty-two, the corner's eight

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.440
seats get counted twice.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.410
Another common mistake is adding an adjustment you

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:41.040
meant to remove.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:43.230
Mark each change with a plus or

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:44.500
minus before calculating.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.280
On an exam, estimate first so you have a target.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.120
Then split the calculation into tidy parts.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:55.210
If lengths or rows change, sketch the old block and

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:56.860
shade each new part once.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.730
Read the question again to see whether it wants an

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:01.640
estimate or the exact amount.
